INSERT INTO feature VALUES('discrete-morse','theory','Discrete Morse theory on finite cell complexes',NULL,'implemented','notable',NULL,'Discrete Morse theory as a library: a complex states the faces of each cell with their incidence and whether a face can pair with a cell, by default a unit incidence, and a scheme states the class of each cell; the check of a scheme as an acyclic matching on the cells a caller gives follows the gradient past them up to a budget, refuses a walk past it, keeps the critical cells and the pairs it reaches, and counts each partner that does not name its cell back, each partner outside the complex, each pair the complex refuses and each cycle of the gradient; the greedy matching on a finite complex keeps a pair only when no gradient path runs back from its upper cell; a matching that the caller states pair by pair, a Tietze move stating one pair, reads as a scheme over any complex, which keeps its own pairing condition and composites, and a cell that two pairs name keeps the class of the last; the equivalence of a complex with its reduct along an acyclic matching works on the complex''s own composites and witnesses, paths and squares for a graph or homotopies and 4-cells for a polygraph: the collapse sends each redundant cell to its partner''s definition in gradient order and each partner to an identity, the inclusion conjugates each critical cell by the witnesses of its removed faces, and the replay refuses a cycle, an unpaired cell, a refused pair, a forged witness, a circular definition, an image that holds a removed cell and a critical cell the round trip moves, each by name; read on its complex the equivalence is a reduction with a witness for each inverse law, and two reductions compose in sequence, the collapses composed, the inclusions composed in reverse order and the witnesses whiskered, with a second reduction over other cells refused at the seam, the replay of the two keeping the first collapse of each seam cell and one inclusion for the round trip and the section of each cell, and reductions of one type sit side by side as a sum, each cell, composite and witness with its part, each part replayed in turn and a refusal named with its part; the reduct along an acyclic matching keeps the critical cells by rank and then by place, with the Morse boundary of each summed over the integers along its gradient paths and the walk of each, and refuses a walk past its budget and a sum past the integers. Doré''s graph `two` collapses onto one vertex and one loop, whose inclusion is the composite of its two edges; two collapses of a triangle compose onto one vertex and one loop, included as the whole boundary of the triangle, whose target is the reduct of the second; the bar complexes of the unary corpus blocks and of the Lawvere theories collapse through it, each face poset''s critical cells give its Euler characteristic, the reduct of the triangles, the sphere, the projective plane and the torus keeps each one''s homology over the rationals, and the abelianisation of each one''s equivalence, its composites read as integer chains, collapses each critical boundary to the reduct''s Morse boundary. The homotopy basis of B₃⁺, its confluences and critical triples, reduces through it onto A, B and two triples, its witnesses the 4-cells of the triples on `sasta` and `sasast`.','theory-discrete-morse','crates/theory-discrete-morse/tests/collapse.rs crates/theory-discrete-morse/tests/greedy.rs crates/theory-discrete-morse/tests/matching.rs crates/theory-discrete-morse/tests/poset.rs crates/theory-discrete-morse/tests/euler.rs crates/theory-discrete-morse/tests/reduct.rs crates/theory-discrete-morse/tests/equivalence.rs crates/theory-coherent-resolutions/tests/basis.rs crates/surface-lowering/tests/squier.rs crates/surface-lowering/tests/collapse.rs','crate-tests','#5507');
